# Mail tool contract v1

## Status

Frozen on 2026-09-29 as `mail-tools/v1`.

The machine-readable inventory is `tool-contract-v1.json`. The Rust authority is
`mail_capability_domain::contract_v1`. Tool annotations are MCP hints for client
UX only; server authorization always follows the security model and
`ActionGrant` checks.

## Identifier rules

All public identifiers are non-empty opaque strings. Clients must preserve them
byte-for-byte and must not derive provider meaning from them.

- `message_id`, `thread_id`, `mailbox_id`, `label_id`, `draft_id`, and
  `sender_identity_id` are scoped to one authenticated account.
- `attachment_id` is scoped to its parent message and is invalid with another
  message.
- `event_cursor` is scoped to account, selected adapter, and adapter generation.
  It is not portable across logout, account switch, or adapter replacement.

Provider-native identifiers may be encoded behind these values, but that is an
adapter implementation detail.

## Sender identities

`list_senders` returns only identities the active adapter reports as currently
available for sending. Each item has `sender_identity_id`, display name,
address,
and verification/availability state.

Draft and send inputs select `sender_identity_id`, never a free-form From
address. A missing, unverified, disabled, or account-mismatched sender returns
`invalid_argument` or `permission_denied` before dispatch. Adapters do not
silently substitute the account primary address.

## Search grammar

`search_v1` accepts a structured query rather than a provider query string:

- optional free text over fields the adapter can faithfully search;
- zero or more sender and recipient address filters;
- optional mailbox and label identifiers;
- read state: `any`, `read`, or `unread`;
- attachment presence: `any`, `present`, or `absent`;
- inclusive `after` and exclusive `before` RFC 3339 instants;
- page size and opaque cursor.

All instants are normalized to UTC for comparison. Naive local datetimes are
rejected. Unsupported search dimensions produce `capability_unavailable`; they
are never silently ignored.

Search results use descending received time and a stable provider-neutral ID as
tie breaker. Search semantics are snapshot-oriented as described below.

## Pagination and ordering

Every list/search page returns `items` and either an opaque `next_cursor` or
`null`. The cursor binds the normalized query, account, adapter generation,
ordering, page size, and snapshot boundary.

Within one valid cursor chain:

- order is `received_at` descending, then provider-neutral ID ascending;
- an item already emitted is not emitted again;
- an item belonging to the snapshot is not silently skipped because newer mail
  arrived after the first page;
- changing any query parameter requires starting a new chain;
- an adapter unable to preserve the snapshot returns `cursor_expired` rather
  than continuing approximately.

`cursor_expired` requires pagination to restart. `invalid_cursor` is never
retryable with the same cursor.

Default page size is 50 and maximum page size is 100.

## Message and thread schemas

`message_v1` includes opaque IDs, thread ID when available, sender and recipient
mailboxes, subject, sent/received instants, read state, mailbox/label IDs,
headers requested by the caller, body representation, and attachment metadata.
Adapters must mark unavailable optional fields rather than fabricate values.

`thread_v1` contains thread identity, summary metadata, and an ordered message
collection or message references. An adapter may advertise `get_thread` only
when its threading semantics meet this contract; approximate subject threading
is not equivalent.

List/search page shapes contain summaries, not unbounded bodies.

## Attachment schemas and bounds

`attachment_get_v1` binds both `message_id` and `attachment_id`. The response
contains sanitized filename metadata, declared content type, byte length when
known, and either bounded inline bytes or a safe file-output result.

Inline bytes are limited to 32 KiB. Larger data requires an explicitly enabled
file destination or a future streaming contract. File output follows the threat
model's root containment, traversal, symlink, size, overwrite, and partial-file
rules.

## Draft schemas

`draft_create_v1` requires a client-generated `request_id` used as the draft
creation idempotency identity. It accepts sender identity, normalized recipient
sets, subject, body variant, and bounded attachment references/uploads.

`draft_update_v1` requires `draft_id` and an optimistic version token. Conflict
with a newer revision returns `conflict`; an adapter may not overwrite a newer
draft silently.

`read_draft` is read-only. `discard_draft` is reversible for contract purposes
only when the adapter has a documented recovery path; otherwise that adapter
must omit the capability.

Draft creation/update never sends mail.

## Send, reply, reply-all, and forward

All external-side-effect inputs carry a client-generated `idempotency_key`.
Adapters persist or map that identity before dispatch when their protocol allows
it. Repeating an already completed key returns the original outcome rather than
creating a second delivery.

A transport loss after dispatch returns `ambiguous_outcome` unless delivery can
be resolved safely. A new idempotency key is a new side-effect request.

`reply` targets the original sender according to normalized message metadata.
`reply_all` derives recipients from the original To/Cc sets, excludes the
selected sender identity, de-duplicates normalized addresses, and exposes the
resolved recipient set for confirmation before dispatch.

`forward` creates new explicit recipients and never inherits original recipients
implicitly.

## Read state and mailbox actions

`mark_read` and `mark_unread` are separate tools. Label addition and removal are
separate explicit operations. Archive/unarchive, move, trash, and restore are
separate reversible capabilities so adapters cannot collapse distinct provider
semantics into one generic mutation.

Permanent `delete_messages` is present in the frozen inventory only so its name
and destructive classification cannot drift. It is disabled by default and has
no executable `ActionGrant`.

## Batch shape

Batch mutations accept at most 100 unique message IDs. Duplicate input IDs are
rejected as `invalid_argument` rather than executed twice.

`batch_v1` returns one result per input ID in input order. Each item contains
its
ID plus either success metadata or a structured error. One item failing does not
roll back unrelated successful items unless the tool explicitly documents an
atomic provider operation.

A retry targets only unresolved items and remains subject to that tool's
idempotency contract.

## Change waiting

`change_wait_v1` accepts an optional event cursor and a bounded wait duration.
The maximum wait is 30 seconds. The result returns ordered changes plus the next
cursor even when no changes occurred.

A cursor gap, adapter generation change, or inability to resume exactly returns
`cursor_expired` or a typed compatibility error. The server does not silently
skip forward. `wait_for_changes` is bounded request/response behavior, not an
implicit forever-running public listener.

## Error envelope

Every failure has a stable code, safe message, optional retry-after duration,
and optional per-item context made only from diagnostic-allowlisted fields.
Raw provider payloads are never returned as errors.

Version one codes are:

- `authentication_required`, `session_expired`: reauthenticate first;
- `permission_denied`, `capability_unavailable`, `not_found`,
  `invalid_argument`, `invalid_cursor`, `conflict`, `compatibility_error`,
  `size_limit`, `unsafe_path`, `destructive_disabled`: do not auto-retry;
- `cursor_expired`: restart pagination or event observation;
- `rate_limited`, `transient_provider`: the identical request may retry within
  the workflow's bounded retry policy;
- `ambiguous_outcome`: resolve state before another external side effect.

## MCP annotations

The JSON fixture serializes all four MCP annotation hints:

- read-only tools set `readOnlyHint=true`;
- permanent deletion sets `destructiveHint=true` but remains disabled;
- tools whose identical request identity can be repeated safely set
  `idempotentHint=true`;
- all mail tools set `openWorldHint=true` because they interact with externally
  mutable mailbox state.

Hints are not authorization. A malicious or old client can ignore them, so the
server independently checks account binding, capability availability, and the
exact action grant.

## Compatibility with Gmail-like mail tooling

Version one intentionally follows the useful cross-provider surface rather than
Gmail-specific resource shapes. Every operation is required to pass direct
Proton protocol fixtures before it is advertised:

- Search/list/get messages and attachments preserve provider-neutral IDs,
  pagination, headers, and bounded payload rules.
- Search/list/get threads require exact Proton conversation semantics rather
  than
  reconstructing threads heuristically.
- Mailboxes and labels map only where Proton semantics can be represented
  without
  losing identity or recovery behavior.
- Sender identities remain explicit verified IDs rather than free-form From
  values.
- Draft CRUD remains separate from send, reply, reply-all, and forward.
- Read state, labels, archive, move, trash, and restore remain separate
  reversible
  mutations.
- Wait-for-changes requires a bounded resumable cursor with gap detection.
- Permanent delete remains disabled even if the provider protocol supports it.

"Required" means the direct release adapter must prove the behavior before MCP
advertises the corresponding tool. Unsupported semantics are omitted rather than
approximated through a second transport.

## Versioning

Any change to tool names, identifier scope, schema meaning, ordering, cursor
semantics, safety class, error retry class, or annotations requires either a
reviewed backward-compatible extension or a new contract version.

The golden JSON fixture and inventory-equality test make accidental drift a test
failure before transport or adapter code can observe it.
